.TH CLOG_OPEN_FD_MP 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_open_fd_mp, clog_open_fd \- create a logger writing to an existing file descriptor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_open_fd_mp(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                  ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline clog clog_open_fd(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to an already-open file descriptor.
The logger does not own the file descriptor, so clog_close() does NOT
close it.  A logger that sits on an fd offers no log rotation.

Use fd=1 for stdout and fd=2 for stderr.  Any other open writable fd
works too, including a pipe and a connected socket; see
clog_set_format(3) for the CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG case.  When the peer end of
fd goes away later, a write after that fails and its record is lost, and
by default it does not stop the process either: the logger writes to a
socket with MSG_NOSIGNAL, and it keeps SIGPIPE from a pipe away from
the process.  For a pipe, the first write from a thread other than
the async writer thread sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN for the process, when
its disposition is still SIG_DFL.  Opening the logger changes nothing,
and a logger on a regular file never touches SIGPIPE.  See
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3) for the details, and for the policy that
leaves SIGPIPE entirely to the application.

You can open fd in blocking or in non-blocking mode.  When a write on a
non-blocking fd reports EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK, the calling thread waits
for fd to become writable again and tries again, instead of dropping
the rest of the record.  The library drops a record only on a write
error that it truly cannot recover from.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
fd         Open, writable file descriptor. Must be >= 0.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped. Use
           CLOG_OFF to suppress all output except CLOG_FATAL, which
           is always written.
async_cfg  The configuration for async logging, or NULL for
           synchronous logging.  When it is not NULL, a log_*() call
           formats only its own message and gives the rest to a
           dedicated writer thread, and all loggers that clog_derive()
           makes from this logger also log asynchronously.  See
           clog_async_cfg_t below.  Unlike a rotation config, a
           struct of all zeros also turns async mode on, with each
           zero field getting its own default; only a literal NULL
           keeps the logging synchronous.
             clog_async_cfg_t fields:
               queue_size        - the capacity of the message
                                    queue.  0 means an unbounded
                                    queue that never blocks a caller
                                    on a send, and a value above 0
                                    means a bounded queue of that
                                    many messages.  A full bounded
                                    queue BLOCKS the caller until
                                    space frees up; it never drops a
                                    message.
               flush_buffer_size - a count of bytes: the writer
                                    thread flushes once its
                                    aggregation buffer reaches this
                                    size.  0 gives
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_BUFFER_SIZE.
                                    The library has an internal cap
                                    of 16 MiB for each record, but a
                                    value above that cap still
                                    applies, and the growth ceiling
                                    of the aggregation buffer follows
                                    it.
               flush_interval_us - a uint64_t count of
                                    microseconds: the writer thread
                                    flushes at least this often, even
                                    when the buffer has not reached
                                    flush_buffer_size.  0 gives
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_INTERVAL_US
                                    (200 ms).  Every value of the
                                    type is valid.  UINT64_MAX, or
                                    any interval too long for the
                                    clock to reach, turns the time
                                    trigger off: only
                                    flush_buffer_size, clog_flush()
                                    and clog_close() start a flush,
                                    and an idle writer thread sleeps
                                    until a record arrives.
mprocs     A custom allocator, which holds function pointers for
           malloc, free, calloc and realloc.  Pass NULL to use the
           default system allocator.  All four pointers must be set
           when mprocs is not NULL; a struct that is only part filled
           makes the call return CLOG_INVALID and creates no logger.
           The library copies the struct internally, so the caller
           can free it after this call.  All loggers that
           clog_derive() makes from this logger use the same
           allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new logger handle on success.
Returns CLOG_INVALID when fd is negative, closed, read-only or
otherwise not valid, when mprocs is not valid, or when an allocation
of memory fails.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library does not duplicate the fd, so the caller must keep it open
for the whole life of the logger.
Every public function is fully thread-safe.  One lock, which the root
logger and every logger derived from it share, serialises every write
to a given target.
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with clogger:
  - call fork(2) before you open a logger;
  - open a logger, call fork(2), and then call exec(3) immediately.
A process that continues with no exec(3) while a handle from before
the fork is live does not use a supported pattern.

As a defense in depth, a handle that is open at the time of such a
fork is technically usable in the child: log, derive and close
operations on that handle work correctly on both sides.  Because the
async writer thread of a logger does not exist in a forked child, an
inherited handle with async mode on changes to synchronous logging in
the child.  This change is transparent.

This fallback is part of the fork protection of the library, which a
build with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 does not include; such a
process must not fork while it holds a live async logger.

When async_cfg is not NULL, CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG output is exempt from
batching, whatever queue_size, flush_buffer_size and
flush_interval_us say: every record and every backtrace frame still
keeps its own write() call.  Every format is exempt from batching on a
message-oriented socket (a SOCK_DGRAM, SOCK_SEQPACKET or SOCK_RDM
socket, such as a UDP socket or a Unix datagram socket), whose kind
the logger checks when it opens.  Such a socket delivers each write as
one message, so every record goes out as one message of its own,
exactly as a synchronous logger sends it.  A record larger than the
socket can carry fails alone: the library writes a "log record
truncated: 0 of <m> bytes written" record in its place, and the
records after it are still delivered.  A socket whose receiver has no
room makes the write wait for room, both where the system blocks the
send and where it refuses the send with ENOBUFS, as FreeBSD does for a
Unix datagram socket; the logger sends again until the message fits,
and a CLOG_FATAL call or the drain at exit waits only within its time
budget.  A CLOG_FATAL call always goes past the queue: it drains
everything that earlier calls already queued, then writes the fatal
record itself synchronously, and only then stops the process.

When a process ends with exit(3), or a return from main(), while an
async logger is still open, an exit handler that the library registers
when it loads drains every async logger of the process as a CLOG_FATAL
call does: the queue first, then the batch, with a "log record
truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): the
process stopped" record for what cannot be delivered.  The handler
runs after every exit handler that the application registers later,
so a record that such a handler logs is delivered too.  A budget of 5
seconds bounds the whole drain, so a descriptor that takes nothing
more, or threads that keep logging, delay the exit by that much at
most.  The budget also bounds every lock that the drain takes: a
logger whose lock another thread holds past the budget is skipped, and
the records that it still holds can be lost.  Every write of the drain
is bounded as well.  A socket is written with MSG_DONTWAIT; on macOS,
whose send(2) ignores that flag, a stream socket in blocking mode is
polled for room before each write of at most SO_SNDLOWAT bytes.  A
pipe or a FIFO is polled for room before each write of at most
PIPE_BUF bytes.  A terminal is written through a second, non-blocking
open of the same device, because room on a terminal does not mean room
for a whole write; the descriptor of the logger keeps its own mode.  So
a descriptor in blocking mode whose reader stopped reading holds up the
exit no longer than the budget allows.  The drain takes the lock of a
logger only when that logger has a queue or a compression to drain, so
a thread that blocks for ever inside a write of a synchronous logger
delays no exit and no CLOG_FATAL call.
When the writer thread of a logger reaches exit(3), from inside a
custom allocator, the drain of that one logger is skipped, because
that thread cannot serve it.  A record that an async logger receives
after its drain (from an exit handler that runs after the one of the
library, or from a destructor) is written synchronously by the thread
that logs it, and clog_close(3) from there still works.  A logger
whose drain the budget cut off keeps its queue, and such a record can
then be lost.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no exit handler;
call clog_flush(3) or clog_close(3) first where the queued records
matter.

The kernel can accept only part of a write, for example when a peer
goes away in the middle of a record or a filesystem has just run out
of space.  The bytes that it did take then go out with no terminating
newline for that record.  A synchronous logger writes that newline
itself, so the next record starts a line of its own and nothing joins
it onto the fragment; the logger then writes a "log record truncated:
<n> of <m> bytes written" record that names the loss.  An async logger
keeps the bytes that the write did not take in its batching buffer and
offers them again on the next flush.  Because they are the exact
continuation of what did get out, the logger completes the record and
delivers every record behind it once the condition clears.

A flush keeps the undelivered bytes only after an error that a later
attempt can outlast: ENOSPC, EDQUOT, EFBIG, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM, or EAGAIN
or EINTR that ends a wait.  Any other write error, such as EPIPE,
ECONNRESET or EBADF, fails the same way on every retry, so the flush
drops the undelivered bytes and writes a "log record truncated:
undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>"
record in their place, and the records after it go out as usual.  The
last flush of clog_close(3) has no later retry, so what it cannot
deliver is named the same way, with "the logger closed" as the reason.

Some records have to reach the descriptor before those bytes do, and
the library never writes such a record between the two halves of the
record that those bytes belong to.  There are two such cases: a
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG record, which the library never batches, and a record
that takes the synchronous write path.  Each one first gives the
outstanding bytes one more attempt at delivery of its own.  When the
descriptor still will not take them, it gives up on them and on every
remaining line of the split record, backtrace lines included, and then
writes the missing newline of the split record (when a line is open)
and a "log record truncated: <n> undelivered bytes dropped" record in
their place.  A logfmt record whose backtrace lines are still
outstanding counts as split even when the bytes already written end
with the newline of its first line.  The library keeps the records
that the batch holds behind the split one, and a later flush delivers
them, so they follow the record that it had to write past them.
.fi
